WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:02.804
A power monitor faces a dilemma.

00:00:02.804 --> 00:00:06.042
Ignore the constant ripple from a converter.

00:00:06.042 --> 00:00:10.088
yet still catch the brownout that would corrupt memory.

00:00:11.138 --> 00:00:17.986
Each rail is watched through its own precision divider, so the threshold barely moves when the temperature does.

00:00:19.036 --> 00:00:23.237
A fault has to last longer than 8 microseconds to count.

00:00:23.237 --> 00:00:26.313
Converter ripple dies inside that window.

00:00:26.313 --> 00:00:28.414
A real brownout survives it.

00:00:29.464 --> 00:00:35.498
The fault is latched and recorded, the warning pin drops first, and the reset follows.

00:00:35.498 --> 00:00:37.603
A fault cannot pass unnoticed.

00:00:38.653 --> 00:00:45.010
One trimmed reference sets every threshold, adjusted once at test to a fraction of a percent.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.591
The watchdog guards the processor, not the rails.

00:00:49.591 --> 00:00:54.059
A kick too early, or never at all, and the processor restarts.

00:00:55.109 --> 00:00:56.753
SKU-6 is pre-silicon.

00:00:56.753 --> 00:01:01.294
None of these thresholds has been measured on silicon yet.

00:01:01.294 --> 00:01:04.818
Bring us your rails and your brownout budget.

00:01:04.818 --> 00:01:07.871
We will tell you what SKU-6 must prove.
